# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0

config MESHTASTIC_BOOTLOG
	bool "Boot history in retained RAM (why did it reboot?)"
	default y
	help
	  Records one small entry per boot in memory that survives a warm reset:
	  a boot counter, the hardware reset cause where the SoC will give one,
	  and how long the previous run lasted.

	  The load-bearing part is not the reset-cause register — on some boards
	  the bootloader consumes it before the application runs — but whether
	  the retained memory validated at all. If it did, the CPU restarted and
	  the RAM rail held: a watchdog, a fault, a software reboot. If it did
	  not, power was lost. That distinction is otherwise unavailable, and
	  without it a soak cannot tell a firmware hang from a power event.

	  Costs about 100 bytes of retained RAM and a handful of stores per boot.

if MESHTASTIC_BOOTLOG

config MESHTASTIC_BOOTLOG_ENTRIES
	int "Boots retained"
	default 8
	range 2 64
	help
	  History depth. Twelve bytes each. Eight covers a boot loop long enough
	  to see its shape without keeping a log nobody reads.

config MESHTASTIC_BOOTLOG_DURABLE
	bool "Also keep the boot history in flash (survives power loss)"
	depends on SETTINGS
	default y
	help
	  The retained-RAM history above reports a power event by ABSENCE: the
	  magic fails to validate and everything before it is gone. You learn
	  that power was lost and nothing else — not when, not how often, not how
	  long the node had been up first. The rate is usually most of the signal.

	  That gap is structural on a LoRa+BLE-only fleet, not merely untidy. The
	  only way to recover a node that has stopped answering both bearers is to
	  carry it to a USB host, which removes power, which clears the retained
	  RAM — so the recovery destroys the evidence every single time, and
	  "no crash recorded" becomes indistinguishable from "the rescue erased
	  it". A node that fails in the field is un-diagnosable by construction.

	  This writes one record per boot into the settings partition, so the
	  history outlives the power cycle. Costs one small NVS write per boot —
	  a handful of records per power cycle, which is nothing against NVS's
	  sector budget — plus the RAM to cache the ring for appending.

config MESHTASTIC_BOOTLOG_DURABLE_ENTRIES
	int "Boots retained in flash"
	depends on MESHTASTIC_BOOTLOG_DURABLE
	default 16
	range 4 64
	help
	  Sixteen bytes each, held both in flash and in a RAM cache used to
	  append. Sixteen entries is 256 B of each and covers enough power cycles
	  to show whether resets are clustering.

endif # MESHTASTIC_BOOTLOG

config MESHTASTIC_FAULTMARK
	bool "Read the fixed-address fault record a reverted test image left (shell: faultmark)"
	default y if SOC_NRF52840
	depends on SOC_NRF52840 && SHELL
	help
	  A test image (an MCUboot test boot that never confirms) that faults
	  before it has any console can still say where it died: its fatal-error
	  handler writes a record to a fixed RAM address that survives the warm
	  reset, MCUboot reverts, and this command prints it. Contract and address:
	  include/zephyr/meshtastic/faultmark.h.
